<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">The Canterbury Tales</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1387–<a href="/years/1400">1400</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canterbury">Canterbury</a> Tales is a collection of stories in Middle English verse and prose by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geoffrey-chaucer">Geoffrey Chaucer</a>, written in the late fourteenth century and left unfinished at his death in 1400. It presents a group of pilgrims travelling from Southwark to the shrine of Thomas Becket at Canterbury, each telling tales in a storytelling contest. It was among the first books printed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-caxton">William Caxton</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/the-canterbury-tales.jpg" alt="The Canterbury Tales" width="320" height="248" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: DemonDays64 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Canterbury_Tales_characters_around_table_%E2%80%94_traced.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
